## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of per-tenant QoS, priority queuing, and backpressure handling under load. It tests the ability to design clear mappings between business tiers and model variants while maintaining predictable system behavior under contention.

## Key Concepts

- **Per-tenant tier mapping to model variants**: Bronze maps to fast models for low-latency use cases, silver to balanced models for general-purpose work, and gold to accurate models for high-fidelity responses.
- **Bounded per-tenant queue**: Limits queue depth per tenant (e.g., max 5 requests) to prevent unbounded latency growth and ensure fair resource allocation.
- **Priority-based routing under system pressure**: Requests are processed in priority order (gold > silver > bronze), ensuring higher-value tenants receive preferential treatment during contention.
- **Deterministic fallback and observability**: When a preferred model exceeds latency thresholds, the system deterministically falls back to the next available tier, providing predictable behavior and enabling reliable monitoring.

## Code Example

```javascript
class Router {
  constructor() {
    this.queues = new Map();
    this.maxPerTenant = 5;
    this.tierPriority = { gold: 0, silver: 1, bronze: 2 };
    this.tierModel = { bronze: 'fast', silver: 'balanced', gold: 'accurate' };
  }

  selectModel(tenant) {
    const tier = tenant.tier;
    const queueDepth = this.queues.get(tenant.id)?.size || 0;
    if (queueDepth >= this.maxPerTenant) {
      return this.fallback(tier);
    }
    return this.tierModel[tier];
  }

  fallback(tier) {
    const tiers = ['gold', 'silver', 'bronze'];
    const idx = tiers.indexOf(tier);
    return idx < tiers.length - 1 ? this.tierModel[tiers[idx + 1]] : this.tierModel.bronze;
  }

  enqueue(tenant) {
    if (!this.queues.has(tenant.id)) {
      this.queues.set(tenant.id, []);
    }
    const queue = this.queues.get(tenant.id);
    if (queue.length < this.maxPerTenant) {
      queue.push({ tenant, priority: this.tierPriority[tenant.tier] });
    }
  }

  dequeue() {
    const allRequests = [];
    for (const queue of this.queues.values()) {
      allRequests.push(...queue);
    }
    return allRequests.sort((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority).shift();
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical system design skills for implementing cost controls in a multi-tenant LLM gateway while maintaining tenant isolation and addressing performance considerations with minimal complexity.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant budget tracking and usage accounting
- Budget-based routing policies with model tier selection
- Lightweight data models for scalability
- Simple REST API for routing decisions
- Testing strategies for budget consistency and performance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Data models
class TenantBudget {
  constructor(tenantId, dailyBudget, tokensUsed = 0, lastReset = new Date()) {
    this.tenantId = tenantId;
    this.dailyBudget = dailyBudget;
    this.tokensUsed = tokensUsed;
    this.lastReset = lastReset;
  }
}

class RoutingDecision {
  constructor(modelVersion, promptTemplate = null, estimatedCost) {
    this.modelVersion = modelVersion;
    this.promptTemplate = promptTemplate;
    this.estimatedCost = estimatedCost;
  }
}

// Router implementation
class CostAwareRouter {
  route(tenantBudget, tokenCount, priority = 'normal') {
    const estimatedCost = this.calculateCost(tokenCount);
    const remainingBudget = tenantBudget.dailyBudget - tenantBudget.tokensUsed;

    let modelVersion;
    if (remainingBudget < estimatedCost * 2) {
      modelVersion = 'lite';
    } else if (priority === 'high' && remainingBudget >= estimatedCost * 5) {
      modelVersion = 'premium';
    } else {
      modelVersion = 'standard';
    }

    return new RoutingDecision(modelVersion, null, estimatedCost);
  }

  calculateCost(tokenCount) {
    return tokenCount * 0.0005; // $0.0005 per token
  }
}
```

This implementation demonstrates the core routing logic while maintaining simplicity for beginner-friendly deployment in enterprise environments.

